      "title": "Read me first",
      "properties": {},
      "widgets_values": [
        "# MiniMax H3 -- preset for a 24 GB card (RTX 3090)\n\nVideo **and synchronised audio in one pass**, on one card plus system RAM.\nThis file is pre-tuned: **drag it in and queue it, nothing to configure.**\n\nMeasured: 4:35 on an RTX 3090, 64 GB RAM.\n\nNeeds **64 GB system RAM** regardless of card -- the text encoder is 25.3 GiB\nand lives in RAM the whole run.\n\n## Already set for you\n\n- allocation `cuda:0,13gb;cpu,*`\n- shape 1344x768, 124 frames (5.2 s at 24 fps)\n\nOnly change these if you know why. **The allocation and the shape go together:**\na bigger shape needs more room for activations, so it needs a *smaller*\nallocation, not a larger one.\n\n## The number that matters\n\nThe two loader nodes on the left carry an allocation string:\n\n`cuda:0,13gb;cpu,*`\n\nRead it as: *put that much of this model on the card, spill the rest to system\nRAM.* It is a per-model quota, not a device capacity.\n\n- **OOM during sampling?** Lower it.\n- **GPU idling below 100%?** Raise it, if VRAM allows.\n- **Longer clips need a lower number**, because activations grow with frames.\n\n`python scripts/tune.py` recomputes it if you change resolution or length --\nand **re-run it if you change GPU**, since this number is stored in the file,\nnot detected at runtime.\n\n## Settings\n\n**Length is in frames** and must sit on the 17k+5 grid: 124 = 5 s, 243 = 10 s,\n362 = 15 s. 362 is the model's ceiling; past it the scene drifts.\n\n**Resolution:** ceiling 1344x768, both sides multiples of 32. A 12 GB card does\nnot fit 1344x768 -- activations alone want ~10.4 GiB there. 1080p output is an\nupscaler's job, not this graph's.\n\n**Work at 608x352 first.** Attention cost grows fast with frames and pixels. Get\nthe prompt and the seed right cheaply, then render at full size.\n\n## Prompting\n\n**Describe the sound.** H3 generates audio in the same pass. Say nothing about it\nand you get silence or noise.\n\n**There is no negative prompt.** This runs through BasicGuider with no CFG. If you\ndo not want something in shot, do not mention it.\n\n**One continuous motion per clip.** You cannot fit a cut into five seconds, and\n\"then the camera pulls back\" gets ignored."
      ],
      "color": "#432",
      "bgcolor": "#653"
    },
    {
      "id": 18,
      "type": "MarkdownNote",
      "pos": [
        1440,
        570
      ],
      "size": [
        420,
        400
      ],
      "flags": {},
      "order": 17,
      "mode": 0,
      "inputs": [],
      "outputs": [],
      "title": "Turbo LoRA",
      "properties": {},
      "widgets_values": [
        "## 4-step Turbo LoRA\n\nSampling runs in 4 steps instead of 20.\n\n**Leave `low_vram` ON.** It is not a memory setting despite the name: on, the LoRA\ndelta is folded into the weights once at load; off, it applies as a hook on every\npass. On a quantised base the folded path is the one that keeps skin clean.\n\n**The custom sampler is required.** Video and audio inside H3 follow two different\nflow schedules. A stock sampler oversteps the audio at 4 steps and breaks it.\n\n**Close-up faces are this LoRA's weak point.** These are preview weights and skin\ncan go plastic at close range. For close-ups, bypass the LoRA node (Ctrl+B), set\nsteps to 20, and swap the sampler input to a KSamplerSelect."
      ],
      "color": "#432",
      "bgcolor": "#653"
    }
